rieza arc in apt 1  our main characters are established with  a clear goal in mind revive their  friends with the Dragon Balls on Namek  they embark on their quests and arrive  in time to witness Frieza and his men  slaughter a small namekian village with  remaining low and undetected being the  initial plan of action things quickly  changes Golan is compelled to give away  his position to save a small namekian  child called Dendy this acts as the  inciting incident and climax for act 1  during this same act Vegeta sets his own  goal arrives anomic and destroys a  soldier within the forces he had been  enlisted their old lives are now over  they are rebels working against the  forces of Frieza  at this time - Goku embarks on his own  journey to rendezvous with the group  with the goal of encountering Frieza  specifically Act two begins as all  parties try to gain the upper hand  through force and strategic meets  however at the halfway point there is a  turn of the tables with the arrival of  the ginyu force due to this Vegeta is  forced to join the main cast of  characters to come back this new threat  ultimately overwhelmed however by the  challenge Goku arrives and makes the  save however he was not ready as he  sustains considerable damage to his body  now out of commission goku isn't able to  help Gohan Crillon piccolo or Vegeta in  combating Frieza this leads nicely into  the crisis of act 2 Frieza being much  more of a monster than any of them could  have predicted Goku arrives on the scene  unable to prevent the death of Vegeta  Goku is still not ready  through intense battle and sheer  determination alone Goku manages to  survive long enough  against Frieza to charge a massive  spirit bomb colliding with the tyrant  the climax of Act two comes as Crillon  meets his death at the hands of Frieza  leading to Goku achieving a state of  being thought to have been a myth the  first super saiyan in over a thousand  years goku is now ready the climax of  act 3 sees goku finishes fight with  Frieza followed by plant nameks  destruction ending in a bank Frieza has  been defeated they have achieved their  goal everyone has returned to life all  is good once again there is little to no  wasted motion in this arc with every  instance serving as an important part of  the story one difference I did notice  was the length of the first act of the  freezer typically around 25% of the  story is set aside for such a section  however since this is an ongoing series  characters and motivations didn't need  to be established which ultimately saved  more time now let's take a look at how  the Buu saga is structured and compares  act 1 begins as the tournament is  brought to the forefront alongside the  return of Goku and the rest of the main  casts taking center stage a short term  goal is established prevent Majin boos  release there is an inciting incident  that leads to Vegeta blowing up a  stadium of people things have now  changed and he cannot go back from this  the climax of the first act is the  inevitable release of majin buu into the  world this is specifically different to  the Frieza arc of Z in that the inciting  incident takes place before the  overarching goal of the story is  established to stop majin buu the  beginning of act 2 sees Vegeta take  responsibility for his actions with the  heartfelt sacrifice as Goku is knocked  out his efforts are ineffective however  majin buu persists to buy time for a  contingency plan Goku reveals Super  Saiyan 3 to boo wood as a twist of the  midpoint  he has forcefully taken away to the  afterlife leading to the inciting crisis  and climax of the act  Buu kills everyone on earth things are  dire enter act 3 now fighting in the  afterlife Goku Vegeta and Hercule find  themselves struggling against a pure  evil Buu through tenacity and the clever  use of the Dragon Balls Buu is finally  defeated and the world has returned to  peace with Goku and Vegeta - have you  been given their lives back the most  glaring difference between Nanak's act  structure and booze is found in its  first act  to emic 17% of the story to provide a  set up with a goal and inciting incident  took both forty percent of the overall  run time you might have also noticed  that in my breakdown of the blue arcs  act structure I left out a considerable  amount of fighting events and  information and this is my reasoning for  what I think is in my opinion this arcs  biggest flaw the elephants I omitted are  not necessary to the story to help us  better explore this concept let's first  understand this quote from John Truby  screenwriting book the anatomy of story  he writes any character who's going to  drive the story has to grab and hold the  audience's attention at all times there  must be no dead time no treading water  no padding in the story in other words  the plot should move forward  incorporating no wasted motion with each  scene piece of dialogue and characters  servicing the arcs themes and Meredith  for example in Marvel's guardians of the  galaxy volume 1 the film begins as were  introduced to Peter quill too scared to  take his mother's hand in her dying  moments he flees outside only to be  abducted the first act is spent  introducing us to the supporting cast of  characters and establishes the goal of  the film culminating in the final act  achieving their goal through Peter  facing his lifelong regret learning how  to share pain what might have seemed  like an arbitrary addition to the film  at the beginning ultimately set the  foundation for the most emotionally  charged and cathartic moment of the  story's climax  there was no wasted motion story beats  happened for a reason to serve a purpose  even Peters alias star-lord has  significance a nickname given to him by  his mother communicating to us the  viewer that she meant so much to Peter  giving his characters goofy and  light-hearted demeanour an emotional  weight behind it now that's not to say  the Buu saga 