th the terms with what he really  cares about to lighten up what if in  order to defeat Buu at the end he needed  to do the fusion dance with Goku  speaking of Vegeta  Vegeta is without reservation the best  written character in this entire section  by far his arc begins almost immediately  after he is introduced and but it's  ending has reached its conclusion with  almost no wasted motion much of the arcs  feeling of suspense and tension comes as  a result of the emotional weight Vegeta  brought to the story and once he  sacrifices himself almost halfway  through the emotional resonance of the  story doesn't come close to that until  the ending which again is thanks to  Vegeta on the flip side of that the  concept of majin buu was an interesting  one in some respects he is wildly  different to any other villain Toriyama  had made before him and the idea of  there being some internal conflict  inside of Buu was interested however he  was plagued by some obvious issues some  of which are connected to specifically  the design of his character majin buu  doesn't have much in the way of a  character or personality he reacts  pretty much the exact same way to  everyone  thus as a result he needed bobbity to  act as his mouthpiece who was in love  himself a somewhat annoying character  however I don't think that's necessarily  the reason his character had issues but  more of a symptom of a much larger  problem and that problem being majin buu  assumes many different character designs  and personalities throughout the arc as  a result we aren't given enough time to  connect to the character as well as we  might want to freeze and sell changed  throughout the show via transformation  but their personalities remain  consistent allowing us to identify them  as the same character if majin buu was  just an evil force of nature that is  something that can be worked with  however if during your story your main  villain changes personality demeanor and  outward appearance as regularly as  buddhas then you are faced with a real  issue of villain hot potato resulting in  a climax with a villain in reality we  just got introduced to a woman that also  inherits all of the responsibility for  the acts horrors if we look at the  beginning of the story and put it next  to its ending you would think it's an  entirely different story being told and  that's because it is with none of the  starting cast even present I think this  Eric would have worked much more  effectively if majin buu was a red  herring and Vegeta was the twist villain  of the arc ending as he ultimately comes  to his senses realizing he was wrong  before making the save to undo the chaos  he had wrought on the people he loved  this video has been in no uncertain  terms the most challenging essay I've  ever had to write  requiring me to revisit many of my books  rereading the manga and talking to fans  of the series of the topic the story of  the Buu saga is filled with some of the  most heartwarming awesome and tragic  moments in the series in isolation but  ultimately I think they are dragged down  by the unorganized and incoherent nature  of its narrative  I still enjoy the Buu saga but in my  view it is without question the weakest  arc in the original series from a  narrative structural point of view  despite having some of its most fun  elements Toriyama had a gift for  illustration composition and whimsical  storytelling however for whatever reason  might have befallen his last hands-on  addition to the series whether it be  circumstance exhaustion indifference or  any combination of the aforementioned  it's the unfortunate reality that the  Buu saga could have been so much better  and was in some respects a mishandled  mess thanks for watching  [Music]
 

